\paragraph{NER U-shaped count response.}
NER recall declines through intermediate counts but partially recovers at full-schema, reflecting a mechanism absent in RE: partial schemas force the model to simultaneously detect entity spans and filter by type membership, suppressing recall. At full-schema, type filtering becomes vacuous (all entities belong to the schema), removing this overhead and restoring extraction performance. RE avoids this effect entirely because closed-set classification does not involve span boundary detection. This asymmetry further supports the conclusion that count effects operate through different mechanistic pathways across tasks.

\paragraph{Schema design implications.}
Typical confusability effects (2.2\,pp) are an order of magnitude smaller than count effects ($-$42\% to $-$56\%), so count reduction should be the first-line mitigation.

